Specifics of the Infrastructure
User Access Journey
A user opens their web browser and types www.foobar.com

The browser queries the Domain Name System (DNS) to find the IP address associated with www.foobar.com

DNS returns the IP address 8.8.8.8.

The user's browser sends an HTTP request to the server at 8.8.8.8.

The Web Server (Nginx) receives the request.

If it' a static file (HTML, CSS, JS, images), Nginx serves it directly.

If it's dynamic content, Nginx forwards it to the Application Server.

The Application Server executes the business logic, using the Application Files and
interacting with the MySQL database when necessary.

The response is sent back through Nginx to the user's browser.

-----
Core Concepts
What is a Server?

A server is a physical or virtual computer that receives requests from clients (like users'  browsers) and
provides responses such as web pages, data, or services. In this infrastructure, the single server hosts the entire web stack.

.

Role of the Domain Name

The domain name foobar.com provides a human-readable way to access the website.
Instead of typing the server's IP address (8.8.8.8), users can simply type www.foobar.com

.

DNS Record Type for www ?

The www in www.foobar.com

is an A Record (Address Record).
This type of DNS record maps a hostname to an IPv4 address -  in this case, 8.8.8.8.

-----
Roles of the Components
Role of the Web Server (Nginx)

Accepts incoming HTTP/HTTPS requests.

Serves static assets quickly.

For dynamic requests, forwards them to the application server.

Acts as the front door of the website.

Role of the Application Server

Runs the server-side code.

Processes user inputs and handles business logic.

Retrieves or updates data in the database.

Generates dynamic content.

Role of the Application Files

The website's actual code base.

Contains HTML templates, server-side logic, API endpoints, and configuration files.

Role of the Database (MySQL)

Stores persistent, structured data.

Allows the application to store and retrieve information such as user accounts, products, posts, etc.

-----
Communication Protocol

The server communicates with the user's computer using:

HTTP/HTTPS for web communication

TCP/IP as the underlying network protocol stack

This ensures reliable delivery of web content from server to client.

------
Issues with This Infrastructure
1. Single Point of Failure (SPOF)

All components (web server, application server, application files, and database) run on a single machine.
If the server fails, the entire website becomes unavailable. No redundancy exists.

2. Downtime During Maintenance

Deploying new code

Updating Nginx

Restarting the app server

Performing OS updates
All require restarting services. Since there's only one server, any restart causes complete downtime.

3. Scalability Issues

The system cannot handle large amounts of incoming traffic because:

CPU, RAM, disk, and network resources are limited to one machine.

There is no load balancing or ability to add more servers.

It can only scale vertically (upgrading hardware), which has limits.
